@page "/guide"

<PageTitle>User guide - Copilot usage simulator</PageTitle>

<article class="guide-page">
    <header>
        <p class="eyebrow">Documentation</p>
        <h1>How to use the simulator</h1>
        <p>
            Estimate whether a GitHub Copilot agent task can run under a set of access,
            billing, cost-center, ULB, runtime, and Actions controls.
        </p>
        <a class="button primary" href="">Open simulator</a>
    </header>

    <section>
        <h2>Quick start</h2>
        <ol class="guide-steps">
            <li>
                <strong>Choose a template.</strong>
                Use Cloud agent, Code review, or Chat as a starting point, or choose one of the six
                green cost-blocked scenarios to inspect a specific economic guardrail failure. The selected
                starter is highlighted and the scenario source shows when its fields have been customized.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Describe the task.</strong>
                Enter what the agent is expected to do. The text documents the intent;
                token and runtime fields determine the cost estimate.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Set the expected workload.</strong>
                Select the operation first. The form then shows only the model, token, repository,
                Actions, runtime, and budget fields that the operation can use.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Apply enterprise overrides.</strong>
                Adjust the cost center, consumed pool, ULB, paid usage, spending budget, and Actions minutes.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Run the simulation.</strong>
                Select <em>Apply overrides and simulate</em>, then inspect the decision and ordered checks.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Repeat when needed.</strong>
                Set Repeat task to simulate the same workload sequentially until every run completes
                or a guardrail stops the next one.
            </li>
        </ol>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>Operation-aware fields</h2>
        <p>
            The selected operation controls which guided settings appear. Unbilled operations hide and
            ignore token, billing, runtime, ULB, and spending controls. Billed operations without Actions
            hide runner and repository controls. Code review shows repository visibility because Actions
            metering applies only to private and internal repositories; cloud agent always uses Actions.
        </p>
        <p>
            Hidden values remain in the complete scenario JSON so changing operations does not erase your
            configuration, but the engine does not evaluate values that are irrelevant to the selected operation.
            The explanation beneath the operation selector summarizes the active behavior.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>Understanding workload values</h2>
        <div class="guide-grid">
            <div><strong>Context tokens</strong><span>Total context used to select the applicable price tier.</span></div>
            <div><strong>Fresh input</strong><span>New tokens sent to the model.</span></div>
            <div><strong>Cached input</strong><span>Input tokens read from an existing cache.</span></div>
            <div><strong>Cache write</strong><span>Tokens written into a prompt cache.</span></div>
            <div><strong>Output tokens</strong><span>Tokens generated by the model.</span></div>
            <div><strong>Actions minutes</strong><span>Expected GitHub Actions runner duration.</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="guide-callout">
            The simulator does not infer exact usage from the task description. Enter an expected workload,
            or simulate several scenarios to create low, expected, and high estimates.
        </div>
        <p>
            Repeated runs carry forward AI-credit pool usage, effective ULB usage, included controls,
            spending budgets, runtime counters, Actions minutes, and Actions spending. The run history
            shows where execution stopped and the totals include only successful runs.
        </p>
        <p>
            Each later click on <strong>Apply overrides and simulate</strong> continues from the working
            balances left by previous successful simulations. Select a template or load a saved scenario
            when you want to reset the starting state.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>Understanding enterprise overrides</h2>
        <table class="guide-table">
            <thead>
                <tr><th>Setting</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>Cost center</td><td>Direct cost-center attribution for the simulated user.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Pool consumed</td><td>AI credits already consumed from the enterprise included pool.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>ULB limit</td><td>Maximum AI credits available to the selected individual ULB.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>ULB consumed</td><td>Credits already consumed under that ULB.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Paid usage</td><td>Whether metered usage is allowed after included credits are exhausted.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Enterprise budget</td><td>Enterprise metered-usage spending limit and amount already spent.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>
            Leave <strong>Cost center</strong> blank to simulate enterprise-only attribution.
            Cost-center ULBs, included-usage controls, and spending budgets will not apply;
            applicable organization and enterprise constraints are still evaluated.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>Simulation decisions</h2>
        <div class="decision-reference">
            <div><span class="status-dot passed"></span><strong>Allowed</strong><p>Every required check passed.</p></div>
            <div><span class="status-dot blocked"></span><strong>Blocked</strong><p>A hard-stop guardrail rejected the task.</p></div>
            <div><span class="status-dot soft-stopped"></span><strong>Soft stopped</strong><p>A runtime or client limit stopped further work.</p></div>
            <div><span class="status-dot waiting"></span><strong>Waiting</strong><p>An approval or external condition is still required.</p></div>
            <div><span class="status-dot indeterminate"></span><strong>Indeterminate</strong><p>Required billing or policy information is unknown or ambiguous.</p></div>
            <div><span class="status-dot"></span><strong>Partially simulated</strong><p>The supplied information was insufficient for a complete cost calculation.</p></div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>Choosing the check scope</h2>
        <p>
            <strong>Cost-related checks only</strong> is enabled by default. In this mode, the simulator evaluates
            attribution, ULBs, included-credit controls and pools, paid usage, metered budgets, and Actions spending.
            Access gates, runtime limits, repository policies, and workflow approvals are not evaluated or shown.
            Turn the switch off to simulate the complete operational and cost guardrail sequence.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>Reading the result</h2>
        <p>
            <strong>First failing check</strong> is the stable identifier for the guardrail that stopped the simulation.
            AI credits show the calculated request cost. Included and metered values show how the request would be allocated.
            Attribution identifies the user, cost center, organization, and effective ULB.
        </p>
        <p>
            When a run stops, the <strong>Why it stopped</strong> card names the blocking scope in plain language
            (for example, agent runtime, individual ULB, cost-center control, or spending budget) and shows the
            configured limit, prior usage, current request, and projected remainder. Checks that occur later in
            the evaluation order are not the cause and may not have been evaluated.
        </p>
        <p>
            A <strong>Scenario JSON</strong> badge marks guided settings that are synchronized with the complete
            scenario document. The setting responsible for a stopped run receives a red <strong>Stopped here</strong>
            highlight, and the result card links directly to it. If no guided control exists, the complete scenario
            JSON opens and is highlighted instead.
        </p>
        <p>
            A green <strong>Cost related</strong> badge marks fields and groups that affect pricing, credit allocation,
            cost attribution, or cost guardrails. Unmarked controls, such as the task description and runtime limits,
            do not directly affect the cost calculation.
        </p>
        <p>
            A <strong>Docs ↗</strong> link beside a setting opens the matching official GitHub documentation in a new
            tab. Simulator-only controls, such as repeat count and custom runtime limits, intentionally have no GitHub
            Docs link because they do not represent GitHub billing settings.
        </p>
        <p>
            Guardrail checks appear in evaluation order. Each row includes its enforcement,
            outcome, configured limit, prior consumption, requested amount, projected remainder, and explanation.
            Use the visibility selector to show all checks, issues, failures, or selected categories.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>Advanced configuration</h2>
        <h3>Optional guardrail cards</h3>
        <p>
            Universal, cost-center, and individual ULBs, included-usage controls, and each cost-center, organization, enterprise,
            or Actions budget have an enable switch. Turn a switch off to omit that constraint
            from the simulation; no JSON editing is required.
        </p>
        <p>
            When multiple ULB types apply, GitHub uses the most specific one:
            <strong>Individual → Cost center → Universal</strong>. Use an individual ULB as an
            exception for a power user or for a more restrictive personal limit.
        </p>
        <h3>Complete scenario JSON</h3>
        <p>
            This optional developer editor can change every simulation input, including billing cycles, seat assignments,
            attribution precedence, all ULBs, included-usage controls, product and SKU filters,
            cost-center, organization and enterprise budgets, exclusions, runtime limits, access gates,
            and Actions controls.
        </p>
        <h3>Pricing and policy catalog</h3>
        <p>
            Use this editor to override plans, included allowances, operations, model price periods,
            context tiers, access-gate definitions, multipliers, pool overflow behavior, and Actions runner prices.
            Invalid catalogs are rejected with an explicit error.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>Save and share</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Save locally</strong> stores the scenario, catalog, and display preferences in this browser.</li>
            <li><strong>Load saved</strong> restores the latest local configuration.</li>
            <li><strong>Export JSON</strong> downloads the complete scenario.</li>
            <li><strong>Import JSON</strong> loads and immediately simulates a previously exported scenario.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="guide-callout">
            Simulation data remains in the browser. Clearing site data removes locally saved configurations.
            Export important scenarios before clearing browser storage.
        </div>
    </section>
</article>
